-- title:
--   Diamonds ⟨ d⟩, d≡ 1 (M), fix the gluing torus
-- statement:
--   The setting is the following. Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a characteristic-zero field $L$ which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L$-Laurent series generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A \to L$, equipped with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$, and let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose Laurent series is the image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of $j$ under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81). The curve in play is the two-chart integral model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, glued from the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) and its infinite counterpart; it is assumed proper over $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   Special fibre data: $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra; $C_1, C_2$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral $k$-curves with structure morphisms $c_1, c_2$, and $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of the model to $k$, over $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` asserts that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` asserts that the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced; `hn` names $n$ as the cardinality of its underlying set, and `hn0` requires $0 < n$. Sections are given by $\varepsilon$ over $A$ for the model and $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ over $k$ for $c_1, c_2$, with `hε₁` stating that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   Picard data: $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model over $A$ (a scheme $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a zero section), `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the sub-Picard functor of rigidified line bundles cut out by the fibrewise algebraic triviality condition `algEquivZeroCut`, and `hsm`, `hsep` assert that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change `D.baseChange k` relative to the base-changed curve and section, and `hPk` asserts that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the base change to $k$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection of $D.P \times_A k$. Designations $D_1, D_2$ for $c_1, c_2$ come with their own representability hypotheses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. A morphism $\nu_2$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $k$ is given, and `hν₂` requires, for every $k$-scheme $T$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, an isomorphism between the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂.some` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ and the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback, along the curve-change morphism attached to $i_2$, of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   Generic data: algebra structures $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ forming a scalar tower, where $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`; the hypotheses `hsmL`, `hgiL` state that the base change of the model to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and `hprL`, `hgcL` that $D.P \times_A L \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ is proper and geometrically connected. Further, $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), and $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to the base change of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ commuting with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The finite-chart preimage in $M_\eta.C$ is assumed nonempty, and the chart pin `hMηpin` states that for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra the associated element of the function field of $M_\eta.C$, transported back through $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$, is the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` states that for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ over the base, if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the chart projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by the same composite for $x$, then $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(\,\cdot\,)(g) \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$ for the arithmetic Galois action on places.
--
--   Hecke and Galois data: `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) (Hecke input data along every prime together with, for each $d$ coprime to $Mp$, a diamond automorphism of the function field and a base-change companion over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) and `hcomm` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54) (the geometric Hecke and diamond generators commute). A multiplicative semiring action of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ is given, with `hΓA` asserting compatibility with $A \to L$.
--
--   The special-fibre group dictionary is a datum $G$ of type [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ of $G.J_{0s}$, and a surjection $G.\mathrm{proj} \colon G.J_{0s} \to G.J_I \times G.J_E$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify these groups with the $k$-points of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` state that each dictionary turns addition into tensor product of the corresponding Poincaré pullbacks, and `hproj` that it carries $G.\mathrm{proj}$ to post-composition with the classifying morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom` attached to $i_1$ in the first coordinate and with $\nu_2$ in the second.
--
--   The generic dictionary is a bijection `gpts` between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of the geometric function field of $X_1(Mp)$, and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$. A family $\varphi$ indexed by [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) (the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[X_i]$ on $\mathrm{Primes} \sqcup \mathbb{N}$) gives endomorphisms of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\tau$ assigns to each $s \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ an endomorphism of $D.P$ lying over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the action of $s$ on $A$. The hypotheses are: `hφmul`, each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law of `hrep.some`; `hφpts`, for the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) the dictionary `gpts` intertwines the action of $t$ with post-composition by $\varphi(t)$; `hτ1` and `hτmul`, $\tau$ is multiplicative in diagrammatic order with $\tau(1)$ the identity; `hτφ`, each $\tau(s)$ commutes with each $\varphi(t)$; `hgadd`, `gpts` is additive for the relative group law; and `hτpts`, for $\sigma' \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ restricting on $L$ to $s$, the point $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$.
--
--   The Abel–Jacobi block consists of: `hDL`, representability over $L$ by `D.baseChange L`; a morphism $\mathrm{aj}_L$ from the $L$-base change of the curve to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $L$; the comparison morphism $k_L$ between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$- and $L$-base changes of the curve, characterised by `hkL₁` and `hkL₂`; a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ from $M_\eta.C$ to $D.P$ with `hajbar` identifying it with $e_\eta$ followed by $k_L$, $\mathrm{aj}_L$ and the first projection, and `hajbar_over` placing it over the base; a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M_\eta.C$ with `hεbar` saying that it maps to $\varepsilon$ and `hεbar_aj` that it maps to the zero section under $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$; `hPL`, comparing the Poincaré bundle over $L$ with the base change of that of `hrep.some`; `hajLε`, that $\mathrm{aj}_L$ sends the base-changed section to the zero section; `hajL`, that for every field $K'$, every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of the curve above it, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{aj}_L$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section, so that $\mathrm{aj}_L$ is the Abel–Jacobi map $x \mapsto [x] - [\varepsilon]$; and `hpts_aj`, that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ mapping to $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $\mathrm{place}(x) - \mathrm{place}(s)$ with $\mathrm{gpts}$ of its class equal to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   Finally, the gluing-torus block: a scheme $T$ with structure morphism $t_T$ to $\operatorname{Spec} k$, a relative group law $L_T$ on $t_T$, and a point $j_T$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $t_T$ whose underlying morphism is a closed immersion (`hjT`); bijections $e_T(R) \colon (\mathrm{Fin}(n-1) \to R^\times) \simeq$ $R$-points of $t_T$ for every $k$-algebra $R$, multiplicative (`heT_mul`) and natural in $R$ (`heT_nat`); `hjTmul`, that $j_T$ carries $L_T$ to the relative group law of `hreps`; `hker`, that a point $x$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ over any base factors through $j_T$ if and only if both its image under the classifying morphism attached to $i_1$ and its image under $\nu_2$ are the units of the respective relative group laws; and `hepi`, that the pair of those two maps is surjective locally on the base, in the sense that for all points $x_1$ of $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ and $x_2$ of $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over a common base $t$ and every point $z$ of that base there are an open neighbourhood $U$ of $z$ and a point $x$ over $U$ whose two images agree with the restrictions of $x_1$ and $x_2$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every natural number $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $(d : \mathbb{Z}/M) = 1$, for every scheme $T'$ with a morphism $t \colon T' \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every point $y$ of $t_T$ over $t$, the morphism $T' \to D.P$ given by $y$ followed by $j_T$ followed by the projection $\mathrm{pullback.fst}\,D.\mathrm{toBase}\,(\mathrm{specMap}\,A\,k)$, and then followed by $\varphi(\langle d \rangle)$ for the diamond generator $\langle d \rangle =$ [`ModularCurve.diamondGen d`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L20), equals that same morphism $T' \to D.P$ without the final factor. Thus the diamond operators indexed by $d \equiv 1 \pmod M$ act as the identity on the gluing torus, scheme-theoretically on all points of $T$.
--
--   This is the statement that the diamond operators $\langle d \rangle$ with $d \equiv 1 \bmod M$ act trivially on the toric part of the special fibre at $p$ of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the stable model of $X_1(Mp)$ over $A = \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}[\zeta_p]$, in the form of an identity of morphisms of schemes on all points of the gluing torus. It serves as the torus-triviality input for the special-fibre operator package attached to the Néron model of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, and is used in the construction of the norm-free abelian subscheme and of the special-fibre Hecke–Galois operator data.
